-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/255a4a9c-1f5e-56e5-bed0-898925e491b4
-- title:
--   Connected ordinary part of G[p^v] lies in ker(abq₁)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a positive integer with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and $M/p$ is nonzero; $H \le (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ is a subgroup which, by `hHp`, contains every unit whose image under the reduction `ZMod.unitsMap` attached to $M/p \mid M$ is $1$. Write $R_p$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbb Q$ (rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$) and `base p` for $\operatorname{Spec} R_p$.
--
--   **The place and the model.** `Pl` is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ belongs to its set of nonunits (`hPl`), and its residue field $\kappa :=$ `ResidueField ↥Pl` is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. The hypothesis `hj` states that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, and $\mathfrak X$ is a model `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` of $X_H$ over $R_p$, carrying in particular a curve model `𝔛.Meta` of the geometric function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the comparison isomorphism `𝔛.eeta`, the cuspidal section `𝔛.εinf`, the smooth locus `𝔛.smoothLocus`, the fibre curve model `𝔛.Mfib Pl hPl ρ hρ` over $\kappa$ with its comparison map `𝔛.efib` and components `𝔛.comp … i`, the two degeneracy maps `𝔛.π`, `𝔛.πw`, the Atkin–Lehner involution `𝔛.w` and the diamond isomorphisms `𝔛.dia0 e`.
--
--   **The group objects.** $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`: a point $\sigma_A$ of `base p` over $\operatorname{Spec} Pl$ restricting to the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with structure morphism $\Lambda.f$ to `base p`, a relative group law $\Lambda.L$, a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ between $J_H(M/p)$ for the image subgroup `infSubgroup p M H hpM` and the sections of $\Lambda.f$ over the generic point, and a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ between $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa, \bar F)$, $\bar F :=$ `Fbar p M H hpM κ`, and the sections of $\Lambda.f$ over `resPt Pl ≫ Λ.σA`. $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`: a smooth, separated, surjective group scheme `O.G → base p` (structure morphism `O.g`) with commutative relative group law `O.L`, a Galois-equivariant additive bijection `O.pts` from $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb Q},$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`$)$ onto the sections of `O.g` over the generic point, Hecke endomorphisms `O.hecke S t` compatible with `O.pts` and with the group law, a bijection `O.ptsSp` from the glued Picard group `GluedPic0 κ Fbar O.ssFinset` (gluing data for the finite set `O.ssFinset` of pairs of places, modulo glued principal data) onto the sections over `resPt Pl ≫ Λ.σA`, the degeneracy maps `O.degPts i : JH M H →+ JH (M/p) …`, the components `O.abqFibre i` on the special fibre, and the further data recorded in the structure. The hypothesis `hrep` asserts that the designation $(O.G, O.g, (O.L.\mathrm{one}\,\mathrm{id})_1)$ represents the relative sub-Picard functor of `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along `𝔛.εinf` and cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero): there is a Poincaré rigidified line bundle with that property through which every such bundle is obtained by pullback along a unique section, the zero section giving the unit bundle.
--
--   **The henselian base and the $p$-divisible group.** $R_h$ is a henselian local domain, faithfully embedded in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, with image inside `Pl` (`hRA`), whose maximal ideal consists exactly of the elements of valuation $<1$ (`hRloc`), and equipped with an algebra map to $\mathbb F_p =$ `ZMod p` whose kernel is again the set of elements of valuation $<1$ (`hres`). $\mathcal G$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): finite free cocommutative $R_h$-Hopf algebras $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective transition maps $\mathcal G.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals. The bridge to $J_H(M)$ consists of an injective additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal G.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to $J_H(M)$ (`hΔ`) such that for every $v$ the subgroup `O.finPts (p^v)` — generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes whose section extends to `Pl` — is exactly the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points (`hfin`); a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ compatible with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hS0`); and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ subject to: lying over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho_h$ (`hS1`); the induced lift into the fibre product being a closed immersion (`hS2`); being killed by $[p^v]$, i.e. $\iota_v$ followed by `O.L.schemeNsmul (p^v)` is the unit section (`hS3`); computing the sections of the classes $\Delta(x)$ as $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$ (`hS4`); being a homomorphism, in the sense that for all $R_h$-algebras $B$ and points $x,y$ of level $v$ over $B$ the point attached to $xy$ is the product of the two sections under `O.L.mul` (`hS5`); compatibility with the transitions (`hS6`); and `hS8`, which asserts for each $v$ that the lift $j_v$ of $\iota_v$ into the fibre product of the $p^v$-torsion with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho_h$ is simultaneously an open and a closed immersion, and that every point of that fibre product lying over the closed point of $R_h$ is in the image of $j_v$.
--
--   **Hecke datum at $p$.** $S$ is a set of natural numbers and $u_v$ are $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ commuting with the transitions (`hu`) and inducing the Hecke operator `O.hecke S (U p)` along $\iota_v$ (`huι`).
--
--   **The section $\rho$ and the specialisation hypotheses.** $\rho : R_p \to {}$`Pl` is a ring map compatible with the embedding of $R_p$ into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρ`), and $\Lambda.\sigma_A = \operatorname{Spec}\rho$ (`hσA`). The hypothesis `hsp` (a long list of configuration data, summarised here) requires: for each $i \in \{0,1\}$, each pair of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $y_1, y_2$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` over the base, lifts $u_1, u_2$ to sections of `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` over $\operatorname{Spec}\rho$ which restrict to $y_1, y_2$ along `barPt Pl` and whose images lie in `𝔛.smoothLocus`, residue-field points $u_{\kappa,1}, u_{\kappa,2}$ of the fibre reducing $u_1, u_2$ and sectioning the fibre structure map, closed points $P_1, P_2$ of `𝔛.Mfib` mapping under `𝔛.efib ≫ 𝔛.comp … i` to the closed points of $u_{\kappa,1}, u_{\kappa,2}$, a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ equal to the difference of the places of $y_1$ and $y_2$, and an admissible gluing datum $x$ whose first component is the difference of the places of $P_1$ and $P_2$ when $i = 0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second component is that same difference when $i = 1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose third component vanishes — there exists a section $s$ of `O.g` over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $(O.\mathrm{pts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of } D_v))_1 =$ `barPt Pl` followed by $s$, and with $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the restriction of $s$ along `resPt Pl` equal to the class of $x$. The hypothesis `hspΛ` (also summarised) is the analogous statement at level $M/p$: for the same kind of configuration, with closed points $Q_1, Q_2$ of `𝔛.Mfib` matched to $u_{\kappa,1}, u_{\kappa,2}$ through `𝔛.π` if $i = 0$ and `𝔛.πw` if $i = 1$, with $D_v$ as above and $D_w$ the difference of the places of $Q_1$ and $Q_2$, there is a section $s_0$ of $\Lambda.f$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ computing $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,i\,(\text{class of } D_v))$ generically and specialising, through $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$, to the class of $D_w$.
--
--   **Diamonds, Frobenius, degeneracies, Atkin–Lehner.** `hdia0` states that for every $e \in (\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ and every closed point $P$ of `𝔛.Mfib`, the point obtained from $P$ by transport through `𝔛.dia0 e` (using the inverse of `𝔛.efib`) is closed and its place is the image of the place of $P$ under the semilinear automorphism `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut` of the diamond action `diamondActionModL` of [`CuspForm.gammaLift (M/p) e`](def/CuspForm_HeckeOperatorFormsGammaH.html#L36). Next, $F$, $F^{-1}$ and $F^*$ are endomorphisms of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa, \bar F)$ with $F =$ `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL` (`hF`), $F^{-1}$ two-sided inverse of $F$ (`hFinv`), and $F^* = p \cdot F^{-1}$ (`hFstar`); $\Phi$ is the permutation of places given by `qExpFrobeniusPlaceModL` (`hΦ`), and `hFdiv` says that $F$ sends the class of a degree-zero divisor $D$ to the class of any degree-zero divisor equal to the pushforward of $D$ along $\Phi$. A unit $pb$ of $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ reduces to $p$ (`hpb`), and $\delta$ is the action of the corresponding diamond automorphism on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ (`hδ`). The degeneracy data consist of additive maps $\alpha_i : J_H(M/p) \to J_H(M)$ and sections $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ of `O.g` over $\Lambda.f$ with $(O.\mathrm{pts}(\alpha_i x))_1 = (\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}\,x)_1$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ (`hpull`), additivity of composition with $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ (`hpull_mul`), the specialisation rule `hpullsp` computing `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` of the specialisation of a section composed with $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ as $(z, F^* z)$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^* z, \delta z)$ for $i = 1$, where $z = \Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the section, and the refinement `hpull1sp`, which for a degree-zero divisor $D$ vanishing at $s_1$ and at $\Phi(s_1)$ for all $s \in$ `O.ssFinset` and an admissible gluing datum $x_1$ with first component $p\cdot$(pushforward of $D$ along $\Phi^{-1}$), second component the $\delta$-diamond transform of $D$ and third component $0$, identifies $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}(\text{class of } D)$ composed with $\mathrm{degPull}_1$ with the class of $x_1$. Further, $\bar W$ is the endomorphism of $J_H(M)$ given by a semilinear automorphism `wgen` (`hWbar`) whose effect on places matches `𝔛.w` (`hwgen`); `hUPgen` states that `genOpH M H S (U p)` plus $\bar W$ equals $\alpha_1 \circ O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0$ on $J_H(M)$; and $\sigma$ is a permutation of `O.ssFinset` with $(\sigma n)_{1,2} = n_{1,1}$ (`hσ`).
--
--   **The ordinary idempotent.** $\varepsilon_v$ and $w_v$ are $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with: $\varepsilon_v$ idempotent (`hεε`); $\varepsilon$ compatible with the transitions (`hεtr`) and commuting with $u$ (`hεu`); $w$ compatible with the transitions (`hwtr`); $\varepsilon_v \circ w_v = w_v$ and $w_v \circ \varepsilon_v = w_v$ (`hεw`, `hwε`); and $w_v \circ (u_v \circ \varepsilon_v) = \varepsilon_v = (u_v \circ \varepsilon_v) \circ w_v$ (`hwuε`, `huεw`), so that $u_v$ is invertible on the $\varepsilon_v$-part with inverse $w_v$.
--
--   **The special fibre at level $v$.** A level $v$ is fixed, $\kappa$ is made an algebra over $\mathbb F_p$ and over $R_h$ in a compatible way, and `hrκ` identifies the structure map $R_h \to \kappa$ with the residue map applied to the image of $R_h$ in `Pl`. Set $A_v := \mathbb F_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$. The hypothesis `hsq` is the commutativity of the square expressing that $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the composite inclusion $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to A_v \to \kappa \otimes_{\mathbb F_p} A_v$ followed by $\iota_v$ and then by `O.g` equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the left inclusion $\kappa \to \kappa \otimes_{\mathbb F_p} A_v$ followed by `resPt Pl ≫ Λ.σA`. Finally, $G^c$ and $G^e$ are finite cocommutative $\mathbb F_p$-Hopf algebras, $q^c : A_v \to G^c$ and $\pi^e : A_v \to G^e$ are surjective bialgebra maps (`hqc`, `hπe`), $G^c$ is a local ring (`hGc`) and $G^e$ is reduced (`hGe`), and $\Theta : A_v \to G^c \otimes_{\mathbb F_p} G^e$ is a bijective bialgebra map (`hΘ`) given on elements by applying $q^c \otimes \pi^e$ to the comultiplication (`hΘ'`); that is, $A_v$ splits as the tensor product of its connected and its reduced part.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Let $\bar\iota$ denote the morphism `pullback.lift … hsq` from $\operatorname{Spec}(\kappa \otimes_{\mathbb F_p} A_v)$ to the fibre product of `O.g` and `resPt Pl ≫ Λ.σA`, determined by the two legs of `hsq`. Then:
--
--   (i) $\bar\iota$ is a monomorphism of schemes.
--
--   (ii) There exists a morphism $\mathrm{pr}$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\kappa \otimes_{\mathbb F_p} G^c)$ to the fibre product of $(O.\mathrm{abqFibre}\,1)_1$ with the unit section $((\Lambda.L.\mathrm{baseChange}\,($`resPt Pl ≫ Λ.σA`$)).\mathrm{one}\,\mathrm{id})_1$ such that $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring map $(\mathrm{id}_\kappa \otimes q^c) \circ (\mathrm{id}_\kappa \otimes (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb F_p} \otimes \varepsilon_v))$, followed by $\bar\iota$, equals $\mathrm{pr}$ followed by the first projection of that fibre product. In other words, the closed subscheme of the special fibre cut out by the connected quotient of the $\varepsilon_v$-part factors through the fibre of $(O.\mathrm{abqFibre}\,1)_1$ above the identity.
--
--   (iii) $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{id}_\kappa \otimes {}$[`PDivisibleGroup.Hopf.nsmulAlgHom (ZMod p) A_v p`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L16) (the $p$-th convolution power of the identity of $A_v$, i.e. the comorphism of multiplication by $p$), followed by $\bar\iota$, equals $\bar\iota$ followed by the morphism of fibre products induced by `O.L.schemeNsmul p` on `O.G` and the identity on the base.
--
--   This is the step which places the connected part of the ordinary ($\varepsilon$-)part of the $p$-divisible group attached to the $p$-power torsion of $J_H(M)$, at a prime $p$ exactly dividing the level, inside the kernel of the `abqFibre 1` component of the special fibre, and records that the embedding of the special fibre of the level-$v$ group intertwines multiplication by $p$ on the Hopf-algebra side with $[p]$ on the group object. It is used in [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_units_forall_qc_comp_baseChange_U_comp_diamond_comp_eq_qc_comp_verschiebung_of_ordinaryIdempotent_of_bridge`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_units_forall_qc_comp_baseChange_U_comp_diamond_comp_eq_qc_comp_verschiebung_of_ordinaryIdempotent_of_bridge), in the analysis of $U_p$ on the ordinary part over the multiplicative-reduction special fibre.
